module gunbc.host_operation_exec


// THE DEFECT THIS MODULE EXISTS TO REMOVE, stated once here rather than repeated at each caller.
//
// gunbc.typed_argv_exec exposes typed_argv_exec_over_ssh and typed_argv_exec_over_fleet_ssh and NO
// TOTAL ENTRY POINT, so every caller that wanted to run one operation somewhere was forced to write
// the transport match itself. Six one-tool domain reads did exactly that -- four arms each, the same
// four arms, differing only in an argv row and a decoder. That is the N x M adapter structure DESIGN
// section 3 forbids at an interface/realization seam, and it was MANUFACTURED BY AN INTERFACE THAT
// DECLINED TO BE TOTAL: an interface offering only per-realization doors pushes its own dispatch
// into every caller. Six copies is what that cost.
//
// So the direction is the one section 3 fixes: THE DISPATCH THAT SELECTS A REALIZATION IS ITSELF
// REALIZATION. It sits here, once, peripheral to every domain caller. A caller now says WHERE (a
// transport it genuinely knows -- which host) and WHAT (a HostOperation). It never says HOW.

// ONE OPERATION, ONE AUTHORITY. `HostOperation` is a coproduct of the semantic operations this
// executor can carry, and BOTH realizations derive from it: the argv identity by
// `host_operation_invocation` below, the native call by `host_operation_exec_local`. Nothing pairs
// two independently-chosen facts, so a cross-transport MISMATCH -- an operation whose ssh leg runs
// `systemctl status` while its local leg runs `systemctl is-active`, both succeeding and answering
// different questions -- HAS NO REPRESENTATION. It is not detected; it cannot be written.
//
// AN EARLIER REVISION OF THIS FILE GOT THIS WRONG AND IT IS WORTH RECORDING WHY, because the wrong
// shape looked careful. It carried `HostOperation { invocation, native }` -- a string-discriminated
// BoundOperationInvocation beside a LocalInvocation, independently chosen -- and then RECONSTRUCTED
// the missing relationship with a hand-maintained name mapping and a predicate, neither of which
// `host_operation_exec` ever consulted. That is illegal-states-representable plus a hand-rolled
// validator standing where construction was available, which DESIGN section 5 names directly:
// a check that re-states a constraint is a second representation of it, and the fix is a single
// authority the realization is DERIVED from, not a check that flags the divergence afterwards.
// Sealing the record with sole_constructor narrowed WHO could author the mismatch; it did not stop
// the mismatch existing. Deriving both legs removes the second fact entirely, so the mapping, the
// predicate, the seal, the mint and its admit list are all GONE rather than reduced -- and per
// section 4b(4) the mispairing witness dissolves with them, because its RED is no longer authorable
// anywhere, not even in a fixture: there is no second field to disagree with the first.
//
// WHY A COPRODUCT AND NOT A CLOSURE. Three of the four transport legs consume argv materialized from
// an operation's declaration. The LocalShell leg does not: it invokes the modeled operation itself,
// and the interpreter runs that operation's own declared shell transport. A closure would be the
// other way to carry that difference and it is wrong on the one axis this program is about -- an
// opaque closure cannot be inspected, refused, cost-attributed or emitted, so the local leg would
// become the single realization the substrate cannot see, inside the function built to make
// realization visible. The real choice is not coproduct-versus-nothing: it is coproduct versus a
// generic-argv lie (claiming the local leg is an argv execution when it is a modeled operation
// invocation) or an opaque-function-value lie (claiming it is unknowable). The coproduct is the only
// one of the three that says something true.
//
// 🟡 DECLARED DEBT, NOT A MODELING IMPROVEMENT: `SystemctlIsActive { unit }` is a SECOND NAMING of
// `systemd.Systemctl.IsActive(unit:)`, which extdeps already declares with its own cited transport.
// That is the nicknaming violation of section 3, inside a diff whose subject is a section 3
// violation, and it is admitted only because the thing that dissolves it is named exactly. The
// interpreter already dispatches local execution BY REIFIED IDENTITY: eval_service_call resolves
// (service, operation) as a string key against its service_ops index, and
// shell_materialize_operation_argv takes that same (path, service, operation, bindings) triple. The
// two legs want ONE description. What is missing is only a .dag-level way to INVOKE by that triple --
// there is no free-function sibling of shell_materialize_operation_argv, and no generic local
// process-exec service exists in this corpus either (no `service Process`, no local `Exec`; the only
// Exec/ExecArgv operations declared anywhere are ssh.Session's).
//
// 🟡 dissolve-on: WHEN an invoke-by-triple sibling of shell_materialize_operation_argv exists, this
// coproduct is DELETED and both derivations below collapse into that one triple. The condition is the
// builtin's EXISTENCE, not a permission to build it: if the v2 self-host program needs
// invoke-by-triple for its own reasons, the v1 freeze admits it on the purpose test and this
// dissolution fires without anyone re-litigating transport ownership.
//
// CLOSED OVER MODELED OPERATIONS, DELIBERATELY. Every variant names an operation declared in
// extdeps. There is no variant carrying a free-form command, argv or script, so this coproduct
// CANNOT express a local invocation that is not a modeled operation -- the property the corpus
// currently has (local execution happens only through a declared operation's transport) survives
// this cut rather than being quietly opened by it. Adding an operation REQUIRES adding a variant,
// which is what keeps growth reviewable and exhaustive rather than incidental.
type HostOperation =
    SystemctlIsActive { unit: NonEmptyStr }
  | SystemctlStatus { unit: NonEmptyStr }
  | SystemctlStop { unit: NonEmptyStr }
  | SystemctlShowProperty { unit: NonEmptyStr, property: extdeps.systemd.SystemdUnitProperty }
  | SystemctlShowLoadState { unit: NonEmptyStr }
  | HostnameReadShort

// THE OUTCOME IS DELIBERATELY NOT SshSessionExecResult. Three of the four legs produce one, but
// naming a local process observation after ssh would be a nickname in the return position, and the
// raw-process-observation confinement cut that follows this one needs a seam it can seal without
// re-pointing any caller. This type is that seam.
//
// stderr is OPTIONAL because it is not universally modeled: systemd.Systemctl.Stop declares a stderr
// output, while the readonly reads (IsActive, Status, ShowProperty, ReadShort) declare none. `none`
// means THIS OPERATION DOES NOT MODEL STDERR; it is not the empty string, and filling it with "" to
// make the record uniform would fabricate an observation nothing produced.
type HostOperationOutcome =
    HostOperationObserved { stdout: String, success: Bool, stderr: String? }
  | HostOperationRefused { operation: String, reason: String }

// THE ARGV IDENTITY, DERIVED. This is the ONE place an operation's declaration is named, and it is
// a function of the operation rather than a field beside it -- which is what makes the ssh and local
// legs incapable of disagreeing. `BoundOperationInvocation` is not re-minted here:
// v2.std.operation_argv already declares it as an OperationRef paired with declaration-owned
// bindings, and shell_materialize_operation_argv already consumes it.
fn host_operation_invocation(operation: HostOperation) -> v2.std.operation_argv.BoundOperationInvocation {
  match operation {
    SystemctlIsActive { unit: u } => v2.std.operation_argv.bind_operation_invocation(
      at: systemctl_operation_ref(operation: "IsActive"),
      bindings: [v2.std.operation_argv.operation_argv_bind_text(name: "unit", text: u as String)],
    )
    SystemctlStatus { unit: u } => v2.std.operation_argv.bind_operation_invocation(
      at: systemctl_operation_ref(operation: "Status"),
      bindings: [v2.std.operation_argv.operation_argv_bind_text(name: "unit", text: u as String)],
    )
    SystemctlStop { unit: u } => v2.std.operation_argv.bind_operation_invocation(
      at: systemctl_operation_ref(operation: "Stop"),
      bindings: [v2.std.operation_argv.operation_argv_bind_text(name: "unit", text: u as String)],
    )
    SystemctlShowProperty { unit: u, property: gunbc.plans.md_helpers.p } => v2.std.operation_argv.bind_operation_invocation(
      at: systemctl_operation_ref(operation: "ShowProperty"),
      bindings: [
        v2.std.operation_argv.operation_argv_bind_text(name: "unit", text: u as String),
        v2.std.operation_argv.operation_argv_bind_text(
          name: "property",
          text: extdeps.systemd.systemd_unit_property_wire(property: gunbc.plans.md_helpers.p) as String,
        ),
      ],
    )
    SystemctlShowLoadState { unit: u } => v2.std.operation_argv.bind_operation_invocation(
      at: systemctl_operation_ref(operation: "ShowLoadState"),
      bindings: [v2.std.operation_argv.operation_argv_bind_text(name: "unit", text: u as String)],
    )
    HostnameReadShort => v2.std.operation_argv.bind_operation_invocation(
      at: v2.std.operation_argv.OperationRef {
        path: hostname_operation_path,
        service: hostname_operation_service,
        operation: "ReadShort",
      },
      bindings: [],
    )
  }
}

fn host_operation_label(operation: HostOperation) -> String {
  v2.std.operation_argv.operation_ref_label(at: host_operation_invocation(operation: operation).at)
}

fn host_operation_materialize_argv(operation: HostOperation) -> v2.std.operation_argv.ArgvMaterialization {
  let invocation = host_operation_invocation(operation: operation)
  shell_materialize_operation_argv(
    invocation.at.path,
    invocation.at.service,
    invocation.at.operation,
    invocation.bindings,
  )
}

fn host_operation_outcome_from_exec(
  operation: HostOperation,
  outcome: gunbc.typed_argv_exec.TypedArgvExecOutcome,
) -> HostOperationOutcome {
  match outcome {
    gunbc.typed_argv_exec.TypedArgvExecConverged { result: r } =>
      HostOperationObserved { stdout: r.stdout, success: r.success, stderr: r.stderr }
    gunbc.typed_argv_exec.TypedArgvExecRefused { reason: why } =>
      HostOperationRefused { operation: host_operation_label(operation: operation), reason: why }
  }
}

fn host_operation_argv_refusal(operation: HostOperation, m: v2.std.operation_argv.ArgvMaterialization) -> HostOperationOutcome {
  match m {
    v2.std.operation_argv.ArgvMaterialized { argv: _ } =>
      HostOperationRefused {
        operation: host_operation_label(operation: operation),
        reason: "host_operation_argv_refusal called on a materialized argv",
      }
    v2.std.operation_argv.ArgvMaterializationRefused { at: _, cause: c } =>
      HostOperationRefused {
        operation: host_operation_label(operation: operation),
        reason: v2.std.operation_argv.operation_argv_cause_label(cause: c),
      }
  }
}

// THE LOCAL LEG. Every arm invokes the modeled operation and projects its declared outputs onto the
// common observation. The projections differ per operation because the DECLARATIONS differ -- IsActive
// names its stdout `state`, ShowProperty names it `value` -- and reading each from its own authority
// is what keeps this a projection rather than a second output schema.
fn host_operation_exec_local(operation: HostOperation) -> HostOperationOutcome {
  match operation {
    SystemctlIsActive { unit: u } => {
      let result = systemd.Systemctl.IsActive(unit: u)
      HostOperationObserved { stdout: result.state, success: result.active, stderr: none }
    }
    SystemctlStatus { unit: u } => {
      let result = systemd.Systemctl.Status(unit: u)
      HostOperationObserved { stdout: result.stdout, success: result.success, stderr: none }
    }
    SystemctlStop { unit: u } => {
      let result = systemd.Systemctl.Stop(unit: u)
      HostOperationObserved { stdout: result.stdout, success: result.success, stderr: result.stderr }
    }
    SystemctlShowProperty { unit: u, property: gunbc.plans.md_helpers.p } => {
      let result = systemd.Systemctl.ShowProperty(unit: u, property: gunbc.plans.md_helpers.p)
      HostOperationObserved { stdout: result.value, success: result.success, stderr: none }
    }
    SystemctlShowLoadState { unit: u } => {
      let result = systemd.Systemctl.ShowLoadState(unit: u)
      HostOperationObserved { stdout: result.value, success: result.success, stderr: none }
    }
    HostnameReadShort => {
      let result = os.Hostname.ReadShort()
      HostOperationObserved { stdout: result.value, success: result.success, stderr: none }
    }
  }
}

// THE SSH LEG. typed_argv_exec_over_ssh applies the shell-safety allowlist; the fleet leg below does
// NOT, and that asymmetry is preserved rather than tidied. The two legs rest on DIFFERENT walls --
// this one on typed_argv_shell_safe_refusal_argv at the edge, the fleet one on portable_remote_words,
// which refuses INTO A TYPE -- so collapsing them into one pre-check would silently change behaviour
// on both.
fn host_operation_exec_ssh(operation: HostOperation, host: NonEmptyStr) -> HostOperationOutcome {
  let m = host_operation_materialize_argv(operation: operation)
  match m {
    v2.std.operation_argv.ArgvMaterialized { argv: argv } =>
      host_operation_outcome_from_exec(
        operation: operation,
        outcome: gunbc.typed_argv_exec.typed_argv_exec_over_ssh(host: host, argv: argv),
      )
    v2.std.operation_argv.ArgvMaterializationRefused { at: _, cause: _ } =>
      host_operation_argv_refusal(operation: operation, m: m)
  }
}

fn host_operation_exec_fleet_ssh(
  operation: HostOperation,
  target: gunbc.fleet_known_hosts_anchor.SshTarget,
  context: gunbc.fleet_known_hosts_anchor.FleetSshExecutionContext,
) -> HostOperationOutcome {
  let m = host_operation_materialize_argv(operation: operation)
  match m {
    v2.std.operation_argv.ArgvMaterialized { argv: argv } =>
      host_operation_outcome_from_exec(
        operation: operation,
        outcome: gunbc.typed_argv_exec.typed_argv_exec_over_fleet_ssh(target: target, context: context, argv: argv),
      )
    v2.std.operation_argv.ArgvMaterializationRefused { at: _, cause: _ } =>
      host_operation_argv_refusal(operation: operation, m: m)
  }
}

// THE TOTAL ENTRY POINT. This is the whole point of the module: the four arms exist ONCE.
//
// The EmitArtifactThenThinRun refusal NAMES THE OPERATION, which is why HostOperation carries an
// identity and not merely bindings. A generic "refused" here would have erased which operation was
// refused -- a correctness distinction the six per-op copies each carried, and losing it would have
// made this a smaller replacement rather than a complete one.
fn host_operation_exec(transport: gunbc.host_effect.HostEffectTransport, operation: HostOperation) -> HostOperationOutcome {
  match transport {
    gunbc.host_effect.LocalShell => host_operation_exec_local(operation: operation)
    gunbc.host_effect.SshShell { ssh_host: h } => host_operation_exec_ssh(operation: operation, host: h)
    gunbc.host_effect.FleetSsh { target: test.claim.pcb_footprint_witness.t, context: c } =>
      host_operation_exec_fleet_ssh(operation: operation, target: test.claim.pcb_footprint_witness.t, context: c)
    gunbc.host_effect.EmitArtifactThenThinRun { bootstrap: _, invocation: _ } =>
      HostOperationRefused {
        operation: host_operation_label(operation: operation),
        reason: concat(
          host_operation_label(operation: operation),
          " refused EmitArtifactThenThinRun transport",
        ),
      }
  }
}

// THE DECLARATION ANCHORS. `systemctl_operation_ref` exists so the path and service are spelled once
// for the four systemd operations rather than once per arm above.
data systemctl_operation_path: String = "dag/extdeps/systemd/systemctl.dag"
data systemctl_operation_service: String = "systemd.Systemctl"
data hostname_operation_path: String = "dag/extdeps/tools/hostname.dag"
data hostname_operation_service: String = "os.Hostname"

fn systemctl_operation_ref(operation: String) -> v2.std.operation_argv.OperationRef {
  v2.std.operation_argv.OperationRef {
    path: systemctl_operation_path,
    service: systemctl_operation_service,
    operation: operation,
  }
}
